This paper demonstrates how relativistic stellar solutions involving two fluids can be constructed by modifying existing single-fluid solutions. The derivation employs either a uniform scaling or a more general, arbitrary variation of the two specific specific volumes within the respective fluid domains.

The resulting two-fluid configurations are constrained by seven parameters: the intrinsic constants of both fluids, the radii and total mass of the stellar object, plus three additional coefficients that define the specific volumes. A complete analytical procedure is outlined for incorporating such two-fluid models into the stellar calculation framework.

To validate the approach, this study applies the derived general solution to generate two distinct two-fluid stellar models utilizing the known single-fluid Dsariy solution as a template. Furthermore, it explores the thermodynamic stability of these two-fluid configurations by analyzing heat conduction and temperature fluctuations across the interface separating the fluids. The analysis confirms that if the individual single-fluid models are stable, the corresponding two-fluid versions remain stable provided the coupling constant $K$ is positive.

Finally, finite surface interfaces are rigorously proven for all two-fluid models where specific volumes are determined via uniform scaling, as well as in particular arbitrary scenarios for the solutions presented herein.

